From de9cd29b88e109484e3562b45a7aeb39f9d39e21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jky72 <jky72@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 09:11:57 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] feat(chrome): tab pills, window controls and a tab width
 cap

Bring the tab strip and window frame in line with the Kanagawa Wave look:

* Translucent tab bar (tab_bar_bg_alpha) so the background shader reads through it,
  plus the active tab's bottom indicator inset to the flat span of the pill.
* macOS-style window controls drawn at the right of the strip when the native frame
  is off, reserving their width out of the tab budget and out of the drag region.
* Drag a borderless window by the empty part of the tab strip (titlebar_drag
  subclasses the window proc and answers WM_NCHITTEST with HTCAPTION there), with a
  6px edge band for resize hit-testing, disabled while maximised.
* Rounded corners and a 1px border via DWM (window_effects).
* Kanagawa Wave theme. Its `background` must stay equal to ANSI black: par-term
  decides "this cell is the default background" by exact RGB match and reads the
  console default as `theme.black`, so any difference paints an opaque quad per cell
  over the background shader.
* Cap a tab at half the width available to the strip, so a single tab no longer spans
  the whole title row and the height is untouched.

Also folds in two incidental clippy cleanups (let-chain in shell_detection, `Ok(())`
rather than `return Ok(())` in menu).
